    "knowledge_point_id": "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008611/assets/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$",
